# findings-two-node.env — what the TWO-NODE sandbox measured, bound to its subject.
#
# SEPARATE FROM `findings.env` ON PURPOSE. That file records what Calico's `first-found`
# autodetection does, and these nodes are explicitly TOLD which interface to use
# (`cidr=10.77.0.0/24`) because both of their slirp NICs hold the identical address
# 10.0.2.15. Merging the two would be the substitution this repo keeps getting caught by:
# same lab, same Calico, different question.

# ── the subject ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NCS2_CALICO_VERSION=v3.29.3
NCS2_MICROK8S_VERSION=v1.35.6
NCS2_TOPOLOGY=two-nodes-one-private-qemu-socket-wire
NCS2_MEASURED_ON=2026-08-08

# ── the wire ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two VMs joined by QEMU's `socket` netdev on 127.0.0.1 — a private L2 segment between
# exactly those two processes. No bridge, no tap, no host interface, no root. The host's own
# Calico binding was `local 192.168.1.106 dev enx00051b8eb138` before, during and after.
#
# This is the capability the lab was missing rather than a fact about Calico: `network_mode
# = tap|bridge` are the only other ways two of this repo's VMs can share a network, and both
# are root-gated and both change the HOST's networking — disqualifying for a lab whose whole
# premise is that breaking a CNI costs nothing.
NCS2_PRIVATE_WIRE_WORKS=confirmed
NCS2_WIRE_NEEDS_ROOT=no
NCS2_HOST_NETWORKING_TOUCHED=no

# ── the cluster, and the three things that had to be fixed to get one ───────────────────
#
# All three are the same shape, and it is this repo's bug class #1: a record that outlives
# the address it describes. None of them failed at the step that caused them.

# 1. EVERY SLIRP GUEST IS 10.0.2.15. The leader advertised that as its API server endpoint,
#    so the worker's apiserver-proxy dialled *itself* — while `microk8s join` printed
#    "Successfully joined the cluster." kubelet then looped on `Unable to register node …
#    EOF` and the node never appeared. A false success on top of an address that means a
#    different machine on every guest. Fixed with `--advertise-address` BEFORE the token is
#    minted, because the token carries the endpoint.
NCS2_SLIRP_SHARED_ADDRESS_BREAKS_JOIN=confirmed

# 2. THE KUBELET SERVING CERT IS A SEPARATE CERTIFICATE from the API server's, with its own
#    SAN list built at install time. Moving `--node-ip` does not reissue it: it stays valid
#    and simply stops describing its subject. Nothing fails at join, at `get nodes`, or at
#    any readiness check — it fails at the first `kubectl exec`, which is the chaos harness's
#    ONLY dataplane observable. A two-node matrix would have graded every row STRANDED and
#    blamed the CNI.
#
#    `refresh-certs --cert server.crt` does NOT cover it — that was tried, printed a
#    confident success, and changed nothing that mattered. `server.crt` gained the address;
#    `kubelet.crt` did not.
NCS2_KUBELET_CERT_IS_SEPARATE_FROM_APISERVER_CERT=confirmed

# 3. ...and the marker that said so was a MECHANISM claim: it printed because a command
#    exited 0, not because a SAN had changed. The reissue now reads the SAN back out of the
#    certificate on disk and refuses if it is not there.
NCS2_ASSERT_THE_SAN_NOT_THE_EXIT_STATUS=confirmed

# ── what a second node makes measurable, and one node could not ─────────────────────────
#
# Two calico-node pods, two DISTINCT VXLAN tunnel addresses (10.1.76.0 and 10.1.149.0), two
# distinct IPAM blocks, and pod-to-pod traffic that can only get there through the tunnel:
#
#   p1 10.1.76.5 (on calico-n1)  ->  p2 10.1.149.1 (on calico-n2)
#   3 packets transmitted, 3 received, 0% packet loss, avg 0.459 ms
#
# The one-node matrix grades its `vxlan-deleted` row on whether Calico REBUILDS the device,
# and says in the open that this is because the tunnel carries no traffic with no peers. That
# rung was an UNKNOWN wearing a DEGRADED. Here the tunnel carries a measurable dataplane.
NCS2_CROSS_NODE_DATAPLANE=confirmed
NCS2_TUNNEL_ADDRS_DISTINCT=confirmed

# ── NOT yet measured, and named rather than implied ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The cross-node CHAOS ROWS are not written yet. The capability exists and is proven; the
# faults that need it — deleting the tunnel under a live peer, moving a node's chosen address
# while another node is routing to it (F.6 with a witness) — are the next increment. Nothing
# here claims they have been run. The two rows are named individually, with why each needs a
# peer, in README.md's "What it does not yet do — the deferred work".
NCS2_CROSS_NODE_CHAOS_ROWS=not-yet-written
